<blockquote class="uncited"><div>I can't see how it will work. When the CPU pull the EN down there will be a current drawn pass the CPU pin and I believe that will cause some kind of damage.<br><br>Snímka obrazovky 2025-06-25 134704.jpg<br><br>As I said, I'm not sure if the EN on the LP3992 is 5V logic, but the better way to do it in my opinion (If one still insists to use the kit instead of the module) is to cut the VCC_5V and connect the EN directly to the CPU. Again, I don't think it is a good idea, just looks better than what it is. <br><br>Snímka obrazovky 2025-06-25 134704 (1).jpg</div></blockquote>Nope, after more than year and use only EN pin in LP3992 with directly connect to CPU and works great in my GD77. When GPS off in software then pull low, when on, pull up. <br><br>If you use N/F pad when GPS module (in OFF state) draw ca. 5mA constantly. <br>When use EN leg in LP3992 current draw (in OFF state) is some uA because LP3992 draw.<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="http://localhost/opengd77/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=5554">SQ1TAO</a> — Sat Jun 28, 2025 5:28 am</p><hr />
