I can't see how it will work. When the CPU pull the EN down there will be a current drawn pass the CPU pin and I believe that will cause some kind of damage.<br><div class="inline-attachment"><dl class="file"><dt class="attach-image"><img src="http://localhost/opengd77/download/file.php?id=3028&amp;sid=1abab8c2e1d6b22044f4aa876b74423b" class="postimage" alt="Snímka obrazovky 2025-06-25 134704.jpg" onclick="viewableArea(this);" /></dt></dl></div>As I said, I'm not sure if the EN on the LP3992 is 5V logic, but the better way to do it in my opinion (If one still insists to use the kit instead of the module) is to cut the VCC_5V and connect the EN directly to the CPU. Again, I don't think it is a good idea, just looks better than what it is. <br><div class="inline-attachment"><dl class="file"><dt class="attach-image"><img src="http://localhost/opengd77/download/file.php?id=3030&amp;sid=1abab8c2e1d6b22044f4aa876b74423b" class="postimage" alt="Snímka obrazovky 2025-06-25 134704 (1).jpg" onclick="viewableArea(this);" /></dt></dl></div><p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="http://localhost/opengd77/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=5068">KA1CM</a> — Thu Jun 26, 2025 3:23 pm</p><hr />
