		<title type="html"><![CDATA[GD-77 / DM-1801 / RD-5R / GD-77S • Re: GPS (GP-02) kit module fitted in a GD-77]]></title>

					<category term="GD-77 / DM-1801 / RD-5R / GD-77S" scheme="http://localhost/opengd77/viewforum.php?f=13" label="GD-77 / DM-1801 / RD-5R / GD-77S"/>
		
		<content type="html" xml:base="http://localhost/opengd77/viewtopic.php?t=3365&amp;p=37549#p37549"><![CDATA[
<blockquote class="uncited"><div><blockquote class="uncited"><div>Hi, I used the GP-02Kit to add GPS to the GD77. <br>I connected the power control to the N/F pin of GP-02Kit. <br>To reduce the consumption, I connected the EN pin of the LP3992 to the N/F pin through a 560 ohm resistor. The total consuption is thus less than 0.6mA (through the MCU "shorted" 10k pull-up).</div></blockquote>I decided to go with the module (not the KIT) because we already have 3v3. To me, the kit is just a module with 5V0 to 3v3 regulator on it. In my opinion, it is a lot cleaner mod using the module.<br><br>I'm not sure about EN on the LP3992 regulator, Is it 5V logic? I don't think you can pull it down the way you are doing it. As I said, it is a lot better to getting rid of the regulator and N/F directly to the module.<br><br>BTW, I don't think the GP-02 support Active Antenna. I might be wrong, but that is how I understand it.</div></blockquote>Nope, I do the same. I connected EN pin straight to MCU pin and works perfectly more than year <img class="smilies" src="http://localhost/opengd77/images/smilies/icon_e_smile.gif" width="15" height="17" alt=":)" title="Smile"> <br>So use EN pin in LP3992 is the best way to cut power to GPS when is OFF in software.<br> 10k resistor on EN pin it's not a problem for CPU. <br>GP-02 kit have stock a passive antena but... can works with active antenna. GP-02 when turn on have 3.3v on antenna pin.<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="http://localhost/opengd77/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=5554">SQ1TAO</a> — Wed Jun 25, 2025 5:25 pm</p><hr />
]]></content>
	</entry>
